[Zig][powerpc64le] LLVM backend: ELFv2 C calling convention for aggregates

Upstreamable: yes. A portability fix for the LLVM backend, not a local
workaround. Upstream master still has no powerpc64_elf_v2 case in these
switches (related: ziglang/zig#17898).

Against zig 0.16.0 with codeberg commit 9df02121 applied (the PKGBUILD
applies that first); the lowering lives in src/codegen/llvm/FuncGen.zig.
packages/zig0.15 carries the same change for zig 0.15.2.

What it fixes
=============
ParamTypeIterator, firstParamSRet and lowerFnRetTy had C ABI cases for
x86_64 SysV and Win64, aarch64, arm, mips o32, riscv and wasm only. The
powerpc64le C calling convention (powerpc64_elf_v2) fell to the generic
`else`, which passes and returns extern structs and unions as LLVM
first-class aggregates. clang lowers them in its frontend (PPC64_SVR4_ABIInfo)
and LLVM's PPC64 backend does not re-derive that: given a first-class
aggregate it spends one parameter word per element, so any function exported
to or called from C, Rust or Go with a by-value aggregate parameter or return
value disagreed with every other compiler on the machine.

Seen as: herdr 0.8.2 (Rust) linking libghostty-vt (Zig) segfaults at once.
ghostty_terminal_grid_ref(GhosttyTerminal, GhosttyPoint, GhosttyGridRef *)
takes a 24-byte GhosttyPoint (int tag, then a union of a coordinate and
uint64_t[2]). rustc, like clang and GCC, passes it in r4-r6 and out_ref in r7.
Zig lowered it as { i32, [4 x i8], union }, so the callee read the tag from
r4, one register per padding byte, the coordinate from r9, and out_ref from
parameter save area word 8, past all eight GPRs; it then wrote its result
through that stack garbage.

Rules implemented
=================
64-bit ELF V2 ABI, as clang's PPC64_SVR4_ABIInfo implements it (ELFv2 kind),
in src/codegen/powerpc/abi.zig. GCC 16 was checked to agree on every tested
shape.

  Homogeneous aggregates: a struct, union or array whose members, flattened
  through nested structs, unions and arrays, are all float, all double, all
  f128, or all 128-bit vectors; no padding (member size x count = aggregate
  size); 1 to 8 members; zero-bit fields are skipped; union member count is
  the largest field's.

  Parameters
  - integers, enums, bools, floats, pointers, packed structs/unions: their own
    type, as before (sign/zero extension to 64 bits was already implemented
    in ccAbiPromoteInt);
  - integers wider than 128 bits: copied to the parameter save area (byval);
  - homogeneous aggregate: [n x base], so members go in f1-f13 (v2-v13 for
    vectors) and the ELFv2 rules for GPR shadowing and stack spill apply
    (a single-float struct is the n = 1 case);
  - other aggregate of at most 8 bytes: an integer of its size (i8..i64);
  - other aggregate of 9 to 64 bytes: [n x i64], or [n x i128] when it is
    aligned to 16 bytes or more; words go in r3-r10 in order and may split
    between the last GPRs and the stack;
  - other aggregate over 64 bytes: copied to the parameter save area (byval);
  - vectors: 128 bits in a vector register, narrower ones as an integer of
    their size, wider ones by reference to a copy.

  Return values
  - homogeneous aggregate: [n x base], in f1-f8 (v2-v9);
  - other aggregate of at most 8 bytes: an integer of its size, in r3;
  - other aggregate of 9 to 16 bytes: r3 and r4, lowered as { i64, iN }
    (clang: { i64, i64 }; same registers, and the narrower second word keeps
    the conversion from the aggregate in bounds);
  - anything larger: hidden pointer in r3 (sret);
  - vectors: as for parameters, wider than 128 bits via sret.

Code changes
============
- src/codegen/powerpc/abi.zig: the classifier.
- ParamTypeIterator (src/codegen/llvm/FuncGen.zig): a powerpc64_elf_v2 case, and a new `int_array`
  lowering: an integer array that may be larger than the parameter type. The
  caller copies the argument into a temporary of the array type before loading
  it (the existing i32_array/i64_array lowering loads the array straight from
  the argument, which reads past the end of, say, a 12-byte struct passed as
  [2 x i64]); the callee stores the array into a temporary aligned for both.
- firstParamSRet and lowerFnRetTy: powerpc64_elf_v2 cases.
- float_array lowerings take their element type from the target's classifier
  (aarch64's getFloatArrayType does not know vector members).

Not covered
===========
- powerpc64 ELFv1 (big-endian) keeps the generic lowering.
- Soft-float ELFv2: clang disables homogeneous aggregates; not special-cased.
- long double: this patch treats every f128 member as one register (clang's
  __float128). clang counts an IEEE `long double` member as two, so an
  aggregate of 5-8 long doubles differs. Zig's c_longdouble is f128 while
  GCC and clang on powerpc64le-linux default to IBM double-double here, which
  is a separate, pre-existing mismatch.
- Aggregates over 64 bytes aligned to more than 16 bytes: clang caps the byval
  alignment at 16; this passes the type's own alignment.
- Vectors wider than 128 bits are implemented but untested: GCC and clang
  disagree on their layout (clang aligns a 256-bit generic vector to 32 bytes,
  GCC to 16).
- @cVaArg of aggregates in Zig variadic functions is unchanged.

Verification
============
Native powerpc64le (POWER9), zig 0.16.0 against LLVM 21.1.8.

test/c_abi (main.zig + cfuncs.c; Debug, ReleaseSafe and ReleaseFast all give
the same counts):
  unpatched, as shipped:                  27 passed, 40 skipped, 0 failed
  unpatched, powerpc64 skips removed:     29 passed, 12 failed, 16 crashed,
                                          10 skipped (other targets)
  patched, original main.zig:             27 passed, 40 skipped, 0 failed
  patched, with this patch's main.zig:    56 passed, 11 skipped, 0 failed
This patch removes the powerpc64 skips from 29 tests and one powerpc64
exclusion of the vector export tests; "C ABI long double" stays skipped on
powerpc (see Not covered).

A generated ELFv2 conformance suite, checked against clang: 70 shapes, each
passed as two arguments, after 7 integer words (split between r10 and the
stack), after 8 (wholly on the stack), after 12 doubles (f13 and the stack),
between i8/double/i16 scalars, and returned; 840 functions, 8680 checked
lines. Every callee prints what it received field by field and every caller
prints what came back. Shapes: integer aggregates of 1-8, 12, 16, 17, 24, 40,
64, 65, 72 and 100 bytes; libghostty-vt's GhosttyPoint (int tag, then a union
of a coordinate and uint64_t[2]) and GhosttyGridRef; __int128-aligned
aggregates of 16, 32, 48 and 80 bytes; float and double HFAs of 1-9 members;
mixed int/float; nested structs; arrays of floats and of structs; HFA and
non-HFA unions; 64- and 128-bit generic vectors and a vector HFA.
  oracle: everything built by clang 22.1.8 -O2 -mcpu=power9. GCC 16.1.1
  gives identical output built alone, as caller of clang, and as callee of
  clang: 70/70 shapes each.
  With the Zig side built as shared libraries (zig build-lib -dynamic):
  unpatched zig: 24/70 shapes identical, in every pairing.
  patched zig: 70/70 in every pairing: clang calls Zig, GCC calls Zig, Zig
  calls clang, Zig calls Zig; Debug and ReleaseFast.
  Objects from `zig build-obj` cannot be used for this on 0.16.0, patched or
  not, because of a separate bug: some local STT_SECTION symbols (among them
  .rodata.cst4's) are written with st_shndx = SHN_UNDEF, so TOC-relative
  loads of float constants resolve against address 0 once linked, and Debug
  objects fail to link ("overlapping FDEs"). The bitcode of the same object
  compiled by LLVM 21's llc has no such symbols; executables and shared
  libraries are not affected.

